### Host jellyfish advisory (use verbatim as the anchor of the piece)

Full text from the host guidebook, to be quoted or closely paraphrased in the post:

> "The house sits on a tributary of the Chesapeake Bay, so we share the water with its wildlife. In late summer and early fall it's common to see jellyfish drift past the dock on the current; they can sting swimmers as they pass. To minimize the effect, wear skin-covering swimwear — swim leggings, a full-body rash guard, and/or a wetsuit. Searching 'stinger suit' online turns up inexpensive options. Message us with any questions before you come."

Source: `src/lib/guidebook.ts` (TRAVELER_ADVICE[0])
Title as written: "Pack skin-covering swimwear for late summer and fall"
Note for Writer: this is the host's own words. The post should honor their directness — it is not a caveat buried at the bottom, it is the honest thing the host tells every guest.

### Jellyfish — Chesapeake Bay sea nettle (Chrysaora chesapeakei)

- Species: Chrysaora chesapeakei (bay nettle / sea nettle), recognized as a distinct species from C. quinquecirrha in 2017. The dominant stinging jellyfish in the Chesapeake Bay.
  Source: NOAA NCCOS Sea Nettles Forecast — https://coastalscience.noaa.gov/forecasting-and-modeling/sea-nettles-forecast/
- Salinity preference: most abundant in brackish waters with salinities between 10 and 20 parts per thousand (ppt). Most concentrated in the middle Bay's tributaries, where salinities are in that range.
  Source: VIMS Bay Nettle page — https://www.vims.edu/bayinfo/jellyfish/guide/bay_nettle.php and NOAA NCCOS page.
- Temperature preference: thrive at 78–86°F water temperature.
  Source: VIMS / NOAA (via search summary; primary URL is https://www.vims.edu/bayinfo/jellyfish/guide/bay_nettle.php)
- Season: can be present May through October; peak presence is July through September, with August typically the worst month.
  Source: VIMS, Chesapeake Bay Foundation — https://www.cbf.org/resources/august-what-good-are-sea-nettles/ and general search synthesis.
- NOAA produces experimental probability-of-encounter maps covering eight Chesapeake Bay zones; the Potomac River is one of the eight explicitly monitored zones. These are guidance tools, not validated guarantees.
  Source: NOAA NCCOS — https://coastalscience.noaa.gov/forecasting-and-modeling/sea-nettles-forecast/
- Hull Creek is "mostly fresh, brackish" (property description). The lower salinity of the creek — closer to fresh than mid-Bay water — may reduce nettle density compared to more saline open-Bay locations. Bay nettles were documented as "significantly less abundant" during high-freshwater-inflow years when salinity dropped below their preferred range.
  Source for salinity/abundance link: https://www.chesapeakebay.net/news/blog/fresh-water-in-the-bay-meddles-with-nettles
  IMPORTANT CAVEAT for Writer: Hull Creek's specific salinity at any given time is not published data the researcher was able to verify. Do NOT assert that Hull Creek is jellyfish-free or low-jellyfish based on the freshwater note. The host's guidebook says jellyfish ARE seen at the dock. Use the host's statement as the primary fact; the salinity context is background color, not a promise.
- Stings: bay nettles can sting, causing discomfort. They are not dangerous to healthy adults but can be very uncomfortable. Protective swimwear (rash guards, stinger suits, wetsuit) is the standard mitigation.
  Source: VIMS https://www.vims.edu/bayinfo/jellyfish/guide/bay_nettle.php (inferred from species page; no medical claim here).

### Tides — Point Lookout MD (nearest NOAA reference station)

- Nearest NOAA reference station to Hull Creek / Heathsville: Point Lookout, MD, Potomac River, Station ID 8578002.
  Source: tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov — https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/stationhome.html?id=8578002
- Tide type: semidiurnal — two high tides and two low tides per day.
  Source: tides4fishing.com data for Point Lookout, observed May 2026 — https://tides4fishing.com/us/maryland/point-lookout
- Typical tidal range at Point Lookout: approximately 1.8–2.1 feet on an average day; maximum recorded high approximately 2.6 ft, minimum approximately -0.7 ft (full theoretical range ~3.3 ft on extreme days).
  Source: tides4fishing.com (May 2026 data) — https://tides4fishing.com/us/maryland/point-lookout
- Tidal range is modest by coastal standards — the Chesapeake's tides are relatively small compared to, say, the Maine coast (where 10+ ft ranges are common). This means the beach and dock at the cottage are accessible at most states of tide; the water does not disappear at low tide.
  Context note for Writer: moderate range is a guest-friendly fact (you don't have to time your swim to the hour), but tides still matter for crabbing (pots are most productive near slack water) and for kayak launch depth. Do not overstate — do not say tides are "irrelevant."
- NOAA publishes free tide predictions at tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov. Guests can look up Point Lookout station 8578002 as the nearest reference point for Hull Creek. Actual times at Hull Creek will differ slightly from Point Lookout due to travel time of the tidal wave; this offset is UNVERIFIED — do not state a specific minute correction without Will confirming.
  Source: NOAA Tides & Currents — https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/stationhome.html?id=8578002

### Mosquitoes

- Virginia mosquito season: active April through late October; peak late June through August when daily highs are in the upper 80s–90s and high humidity shortens breeding cycles to 5–7 days.
  Source: mosquitalk.com "Mosquito Season in Virginia (2026)" — https://mosquitalk.com/mosquito-season-in-virginia and americanpest.net — https://americanpest.net/about-us/resources/pest-guides-and-faqs/complete-guide-to-mosquitoes-in-maryland-virginia-and-washington-d-c/
- Waterfront and wooded creek locations (like Hull Creek) tend to experience especially pronounced mosquito pressure in peak summer months due to standing water and humid conditions.
  Source: general Virginia mosquito season synthesis from above sources.
- Afternoon thunderstorms common June–August in the region create repeated standing-water pulses that trigger fresh waves of mosquito egg-laying.
  Source: extermpro.com / mosquito season articles (synthesized).
- The screened porches at the cottage are a direct practical response to this — they are one of the property's named standout amenities.
  Source: `src/lib/site.ts` (STANDOUT_AMENITIES, "Two screened porches")
- Specific mosquito density at Hull Creek vs. neighboring properties: UNVERIFIED — do not state as fact. Will has firsthand knowledge.

### Best months / seasonal guidance

- June is the sweet spot most sources converge on: warm water, fewer jellyfish than July–September, lower mosquito pressure than peak summer, sunsets still long. Crabbing season is active (crabs feed aggressively June–August at dawn and dusk).
  Source: synthesis of crabbing/swimming/jellyfish season data; primary sources listed above.
- Late September and October: shoulder season. Jellyfish thin out as water cools below their preferred temperature range. Mosquito pressure drops sharply. Crabbing still possible (season runs through November per VMRC, though crabs move toward deeper water as temps drop). Foliage colors appear. The trade-off is cooler air temperatures for swimming.
  Source: Virginia.org Northern Neck travel guide — https://www.virginia.org/plan-your-trip/trip-ideas/getaway-to-the-northern-neck-and-chesapeake-bay/ and crabbing season synthesis.
- July–August: peak summer, warmest water, highest jellyfish and mosquito pressure. Hot tub and sauna most valuable as evening alternatives to open-water swimming. Best sunset window.
  Source: seasonal synthesis from sources above.
- Exact date when jellyfish reliably clear from Hull Creek: UNVERIFIED — year-to-year variability is high. Do not promise "jellyfish-free after [date]." The host's guidebook says "late summer and early fall" is the risk window; that framing is the right level of precision.

### Crabbing context (for brief seasonal mention, not the main topic — that is the separate post)

- Blue crab season Potomac/Northern Neck: active March–November; peak productivity June–August.
  Source: kayakcambria.com and crabbingzone.com Virginia crabbing guides.
- Crab pots provided at the cottage; run lines from the dock.
  Source: `src/lib/site.ts` (STANDOUT_AMENITIES, "Dock & crab pots")

