## Solid / gaps summary

**Solid:**
- Host jellyfish advisory is verbatim from guidebook — the anchor of the piece is locked.
- Sea nettle species, salinity preference (10–20 ppt), and peak season (July–September) are well-sourced from VIMS and NOAA NCCOS.
- Point Lookout tide station confirmed as nearest NOAA reference; semidiurnal pattern and ~1.8–2.1 ft mean range confirmed from tides4fishing.com data.
- Mosquito peak (late June–August) is consistent across multiple Virginia pest-season sources.
- Seasonal best-months framing (June sweet spot, late September shoulder) is consistent across Virginia travel and crabbing sources.
- All property facts pulled verbatim from `src/lib/site.ts` and `captains_cottage_brief.md §7`.
- NOAA NCCOS sea nettle probability maps confirmed as a real, publicly available tool covering the Potomac River zone.

**Gaps / UNVERIFIED:**
- Hull Creek's specific salinity at any given tide or season is not publicly documented. The "mostly fresh, brackish" characterization from the property description is plausible but not independently quantified. Writer must not promise Hull Creek is protected from jellyfish by low salinity.
- Tide offset between Point Lookout reference station and Hull Creek dock: unknown. Only Will can give this.
- Exact date when jellyfish reliably clear in any given year: inherently variable, unverifiable in advance. Hedge appropriately.
- Mosquito density at Hull Creek specifically vs. neighboring inland locations: not independently documented. Will has ground truth.
- Whether Will treats the yard or uses any mosquito mitigation at the property: unknown.
- NOAA station page for 8578002 was not successfully fetched for datum details; tidal range figures come from a secondary source (tides4fishing.com). Values appear consistent with Chesapeake Bay tidal norms but the Writer should hedge them as "typically" rather than "exactly."
